        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Where can I find a used treadmill near me?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Local online marketplaces and classifieds have the most listings and the lowest prices. Used fitness equipment stores sell serviced machines, often with a short warranty. Gym and hotel liquidations and estate sales occasionally offer good machines cheaply. See any machine running before paying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where can I buy used exercise equipment near me?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Used fitness equipment dealers, local marketplaces, classifieds, gym closures and estate sales all sell used exercise equipment. Dealers charge more but usually service machines and may include a warranty or delivery. Private sellers are cheaper with no recourse.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much should I pay for a used treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As a typical guide, roughly half the original price at about two years old and around a third at three to five years, then it flattens. Adjust for condition, hours and maintenance history, and add $80 to $200 for transport if you are collecting it yourself.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to buy a used treadmill from a marketplace?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, with precautions. Take someone with you, tell someone where you are going, check the seller's profile, test the machine running before paying, and never send a deposit or shipping payment for a machine you have not seen.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I get a used treadmill home?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Plan for a van or pickup, at least two capable people, straps or a dolly and tie-downs. Measure doorways and stairs at both ends, fold and lock the machine if it folds, and consider paying a local courier for heavy machines or difficult stairs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are common used treadmill scams?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Requests for a deposit to hold the machine, offers to ship it after payment, payment by gift card, wire or cryptocurrency, fake payment confirmations and premium machines priced far below the market. Pay only on collection after testing, and keep messages on the platform.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

